CHESS FORGE — FUNCTIONALITY SPECIFICATION v2.0

This document describes what Chess Forge does, feature by feature. Use it to build, test, or verify the app. It contains no marketing, no metrics, no roadmap — only functionality.

---

1. APP OVERVIEW

Chess Forge is an Android chess analysis and training app. It has four modes:

1. Practice Mode — load a PGN and analyze move by move with full game context, move classification, and accuracy tracking.
2. Screenshot Analyzer — load an image of a chess position and analyze it, with batch processing support.
3. Live Overlay — capture the screen in real time and draw the best move as an arrow on top of any app, with turn detection and game reconstruction.
4. Puzzle Mode — solve tactical puzzles generated from your own games, screenshot positions, or engine-constructed positions.

All four modes share the same engine pool (Stockfish 19 with NNUE), the same board rendering, the same vision pipeline, the same game state tracker, and the same analysis output format.

---

2. MODE 1 — PRACTICE MODE

2.1 Loading a PGN

The user can load a PGN from three sources:
- File picker — select a `.pgn` file from device storage.
- Clipboard paste — paste PGN text directly.
- Lichess study URL — paste a Lichess study URL and the app fetches the PGN.

2.2 Board and Move List

- A chessboard is displayed at the top of the screen showing the current position.
- A move list is displayed at the bottom, showing all moves in the game in standard algebraic notation.
- Each move in the list is tappable — tapping jumps to that position.
- The user can step forward and backward with:
  - Arrow keys (external keyboard)
  - Swipe left/right gestures
  - Previous/next buttons below the board
- Undo — the user can take back moves and try alternatives. The original move is preserved as a variation.

2.3 Analysis on Every Position

When the user navigates to any position (by tapping a move or stepping forward/backward), the app:
1. Queues the position with the engine pool (quick-analysis instance).
2. Displays:
   - The best move as an arrow on the board.
   - The evaluation bar (horizontal bar showing centipawn score).
   - The top 3 moves with their evaluations (MultiPV 3, user-adjustable 1–5).
   - The depth reached and nodes searched.
   - Move classification for the move that led to this position (see 2.7).

2.4 Exports

- Copy FEN — copies the current position's FEN to clipboard.
- Export PGN — saves the full game with engine annotations (eval comments on every move, move classifications, accuracy percentages) as a `.pgn` file.
- Share Analysis Image — generates a shareable image (PNG) showing the board position, best move arrow, evaluation, and move classification. The image includes a small "Analyzed with Chess Forge" watermark in the corner.

2.5 Offline

Practice Mode works fully offline. No network, no permissions required.

2.6 Game Context Panel

Below the move list, a collapsible panel shows:

- Opening identification — ECO code and opening name (e.g., "B90: Sicilian Defense: Najdorf Variation"). Identified from the move sequence. Tapping the opening name shows a brief description and common plans.
- Game phase indicator — opening / middlegame / endgame, based on material count, piece development, and move number. Displayed as a subtle colored bar: blue (opening), amber (middlegame), gray (endgame).
- Tactical alerts — real-time flags detected by the engine:
  - "Hanging piece — your knight on f3 is undefended."
  - "Mate in 2 available."
  - "Opponent's last move was a blunder (-1.4)."
  - "Fork opportunity detected."
- Material balance — visual piece differential (e.g., "+N" for an extra knight, "-QP" for down a queen and pawn).
- Clock estimates — if the PGN includes clock annotations, estimated remaining time for both sides.

2.7 Move Classification

After each move in the move list, a colored indicator appears:

Indicator	Classification	Definition	
🟦	Best	Engine's top choice	
🟩	Good	Within 0.3 pawns of best	
🟨	Inaccuracy	0.3–1.0 pawns worse than best	
🟧	Mistake	1.0–2.0 pawns worse than best	
🟥	Blunder	2.0+ pawns worse than best	

- Classification thresholds are user-adjustable in Settings → Engine → Classification Thresholds.
- Tapping a classification indicator shows:
  - The evaluation before and after the move.
  - The best move that was available.
  - A one-line natural-language explanation: "This hangs your knight to Qxe5."
- Haptic feedback — a light buzz for blunders, a gentle tap for best moves. Toggleable in Settings → Board.

2.8 Accuracy Meter

After the game ends (or at any point during review), an accuracy percentage is calculated and displayed:

- Your accuracy: 78.3% — computed using a weighted formula based on evaluation drops per move.
- Accuracy is shown per-game and per-phase (opening accuracy, middlegame accuracy, endgame accuracy).
- Accuracy is included in exported PGNs as a comment header.

2.9 Interactive Engine Lines

Each of the top 3 engine lines is tappable. Tapping a line:
1. Plays the first move of that line on the board as a variation (dashed arrow).
2. Shows the next 3 moves of the line as a text continuation below the board.
3. Allows the user to step through the variation move by move using the same navigation controls.
4. A "Return to main line" button appears in the move list header.
5. Variations are displayed in the move list with indentation and a subtle color difference from the main game.
6. The user can create their own variations by making moves on the board at any point. These are saved with the PGN.

2.10 Critical Moments

The app automatically identifies critical moments in the game — positions where the evaluation swung by more than 1.5 pawns in a single move. These are:
- Marked in the move list with a ⚡ icon.
- Collected in a "Critical Moments" view accessible from the game context panel.
- Each critical moment shows the position, the move played, the best move, and the evaluation swing.

