4. MODE 3 — LIVE OVERLAY

4.1 Starting the Overlay

The user starts the overlay from a button in the app:
1. The app requests MediaProjection permission via the system consent dialog.
2. The app requests SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW permission (draw over other apps) via a settings redirect.
3. Once both permissions are granted, the app starts a Foreground Service and shows a persistent notification with a Stop button and a profile selector (see 8.5).

4.2 Board Region Selection

- On first start, the user is shown an overlay rectangle covering the currently visible board.
- The user drags the corners of the rectangle to fit the board precisely.
- The region is saved and persists across sessions.
- If the user rotates the device, capture pauses and prompts the user to rotate back (the region is invalidated by rotation).

4.3 Capture Loop

The Foreground Service runs a continuous loop:

1. Capture the screen at the configured frame rate (default 5 FPS, adjustable 1–15).
2. Crop the captured frame to the user's saved board region.
3. Compute a perceptual hash of the cropped region.
4. Stability filter — if the hash does not match the previous two hashes, discard the frame. This prevents analysis of mid-animation frames.
5. If the frame is stable, run the vision pipeline (see 4.3a).
6. If confidence ≥ 0.75, send the FEN to the engine pool.
7. The engine returns the best move and evaluation.
8. Update the overlay with the new arrow and evaluation badge.

4.3a Incremental Vision Processing

To minimize latency and battery usage:

- Diff masking — a pixel-level diff is computed between the current stable frame and the previous stable frame. Only squares that changed are re-classified. A typical chess move changes 2 squares (source and destination); a capture changes 3.
- Square classification cache — each square's classification and confidence are cached. Unchanged squares use cached values.
- Previous position prior — the previous position's FEN is used as a Bayesian prior. A square that contained a white pawn should not suddenly contain a black queen unless a capture and promotion occurred in the same move.
- Turn inference — by comparing the previous and current positions, the app determines which side just moved and which side is now to move (see 4.10).

This reduces average inference time by approximately 90% compared to full-board classification on every frame.

4.4 The Best-Move Arrow

The arrow is the primary visual element of the overlay.

Positioning:
- The arrow is drawn in screen coordinates, computed from the saved board region.
- The center of each square is at:
  - `square_x = region.x + (file + 0.5) * (region.w / 8)`
  - `square_y = region.y + (rank + 0.5) * (region.h / 8)`
- The arrow goes from the center of the source square to the center of the destination square.
- Orientation is respected — if black is at the bottom, the coordinates are flipped.

Visual properties:
- Solid green (`#00E676`) by default for the best move.
- Solid yellow (`#FFD600`) when there is only one legal move.
- Solid blue (`#448AFF`) for captures (user-customizable, see 8.4).
- Solid red (`#FF1744`) for checks and checkmates.
- Dashed green if confidence is 0.75–0.89 (mid confidence).
- No arrow if confidence < 0.75.
- Semi-transparent (75% opacity by default, adjustable 40–100%).
- Black outline (1 dp, 30% opacity) for visibility on any board color.
- Soft glow (25% opacity) around the arrow.
- Shaft width 3–5 dp, scaled to board size.
- Arrowhead 6× shaft width.
- Tail taper — slight taper from shaft to arrowhead.

Animation:
- Fade in over 200 ms on a new move.
- Fade out over 150 ms when the position changes.
- Soft pulse (±10% opacity) every 1.5 s.
- No slide, bounce, or spin.
- Reduced motion mode — all animations disabled (see Section 14).

Special cases:
- Capture — arrow drawn in capture color (default blue); small × symbol near the arrowhead.
- Check — arrow drawn in red; `+` symbol near the arrowhead.
- Checkmate — arrow drawn in red; `#` symbol; arrowhead pulsing.
- Castling — main arrow shows king path; secondary smaller arrow shows rook path.
- En passant — arrow drawn diagonally; ⚠ icon appears.
- Promotion — arrow drawn normally; `=Q`/`=R`/`=B`/`=N` label appears near the arrowhead.
- No legal moves — no arrow; overlay shows "Checkmate" or "Stalemate."

User customization:
- Arrow color: green (default), blue, purple, orange.
- Per-move-type colors: user can set different colors for quiet moves, captures, checks, and only-moves.
- Arrow thickness: thin / medium / thick.
- Arrow opacity: 40–100%.
- Pulse animation: on/off.
- Show eval badge: on/off.
- Show confidence dot: on/off.
- Show coordinate hint (small move text like "Nf3"): on/off.

4.5 Secondary Overlay Elements

- Evaluation badge — small pill in the top-right of the board region showing `+0.35` (green) or `−1.2` (red).
- Confidence dot — 8 dp dot next to the badge. Green (≥0.90), yellow (0.75–0.89), red (<0.75). If red, the arrow is not shown.
- Castling warning icon — small ⚠ shown if the best move is castling and rights could not be verified.
- Turn indicator — small text showing whose move it is: "White to move" / "Black to move" / "Your move" (see 4.10).
- Drag handle — 40×40 dp circle at top-right of the screen for repositioning the overlay.
- Minimize button — 30×30 dp × at top-left. Collapses the whole overlay to a floating 50×50 bubble.

4.6 Overlay Interaction Rules

- The overlay uses `FLAG_NOT_FOCUSABLE` — touches pass through to the underlying app. The user can interact with the chess app normally.
- No auto-play. The arrow is a suggestion only.
- No input interception.
- No key logging.
- The overlay stops immediately when the user taps Stop in the notification.

4.7 Battery Management

- Battery kill switch — if the board region has been unchanged for 5 seconds, capture drops to 1 FPS.
- Resume — as soon as a change is detected (pixel-diff threshold), capture returns to the configured rate.
- Screen off — capture pauses immediately when the screen turns off (listens for `ACTION_SCREEN_OFF`).
- Screen on — capture resumes on `ACTION_SCREEN_ON`.
- App backgrounded — when Chess Forge itself is backgrounded (overlay still running), the engine pool pauses deep analysis and only runs quick analysis.

4.8 Competitive App Detection

- The app monitors the foreground package name.
- If the foreground app is Chess.com, Lichess, or Chess24, a small toast appears: "Live analysis in competitive games violates platform terms of service and may result in account bans."
- The app does not block, does not intercept, does not automate anything.

4.9 Stopping the Overlay

The user can stop the overlay from:
- The Stop button in the persistent notification.
- The minimize button followed by a long-press on the bubble.
- The Android recent apps screen (the service is killed by the system).

When stopped:
- The overlay window is removed.
- The MediaProjection session is released.
- The Foreground Service stops.
- The notification disappears.
- If game reconstruction captured moves, the app offers to save the game for post-game analysis (see 4.11).

4.10 Turn Detection and Game State Tracking

The overlay maintains a Game State Object that tracks the full game in progress:

```
Game State Object:
├── Move history (UCI moves from game start or overlay start)
├── Current FEN
├── Move number
├── Side to move
├── Position history (all FENs seen this session)
├── Board orientation (white at bottom / black at bottom)
├── Captured pieces (both sides)
├── Material balance
├── Opening identification (updated as moves are detected)
├── Game phase (opening / middlegame / endgame)
├── Clock estimates (if clock is visible in the captured region)
└── Tactical alert flags
```

Turn detection:
- When a stable frame differs from the previous stable frame, a move has been played.
- The app determines which side moved by comparing piece positions and applying chess legality rules.
- The arrow only shows for the side to move.
- If the user is playing a game, the arrow shows the player's best move (not the opponent's).
- A small indicator shows whose turn it is: "Your move" (white/black) or "Opponent's move."

Game reconstruction:
- Every detected move is recorded with its timestamp.
- If the overlay was active from the start of the game, the full game can be reconstructed.
- If the overlay was started mid-game, the app notes this: "Game reconstruction may be incomplete."

4.11 Post-Game Analysis

When the overlay detects that a game has ended (final position stable for 10+ seconds, or user stops the overlay after 10+ moves were captured):

- The app offers: "Analyze this game"
- The reconstructed game is loaded into Practice Mode with:
  - Full move list (from detected moves).
  - Move classifications for every move.
  - Accuracy percentages for both sides.
  - Critical moments highlighted.
  - Opening identification.
- If the overlay was started mid-game, a warning appears: "Analysis may be incomplete — overlay was not active from the beginning. First N moves are unknown."

